#{[{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046659} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492961} :activity/id #uuid "23e0df44-7023-5786-bcc6-bf2a24b2da0f" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050429 :topic/id #uuid "486a09f3-45ae-5574-b216-0a329beb1dea" :topic/math-academy-id 757} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492935 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302134 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "09623bc9-a857-55e4-9eb6-62ab35d1cac7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "28" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299980}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299980} :answer-field/id #uuid "7f9e6e03-d8f7-519b-bf8f-09b6838f4775" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301627}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "7782f6dc-5bfd-598b-a7bf-b3050e3b4fc9" :question/math-academy-id "q-323215" :question/problem "According to the rational root theorem, the polynomial $f(x)=16{x}^{4}-3{x}^{3}+7x+12$ has{{field-1}} *potential* rational roots." :question/worked-solution "Given a polynomial with integer coefficients, the rational roots theorem states that any rational roots of the polynomial must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of the constant term}}{\\text{factor of the leading coefficient}}.\n$$\n\nFor the given polynomial:\n\n- The constant term is $12,$ and its factors are $\\pm 1,$ $\\pm 2,$ $\\pm 3,$ $\\pm 4,$ $\\pm 6,$ and $\\pm 12.$\n\n- The leading coefficient is $16,$ and its factors are $\\pm 1,$ $\\pm 2,$ $\\pm 4,$ $\\pm 8,$ and $\\pm 16.$\n\nFirst, we list out all the possibilities for the numerator and denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\pm \\frac{1}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{4}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{8}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{16}, \\\\ \\pm \\frac{2}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{2}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{2}{4}, & \\pm \\frac{2}{8}, & \\pm \\frac{2}{16}, \\\\ \\pm \\frac{3}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{3}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{3}{4}, & \\pm \\frac{3}{8}, & \\pm \\frac{3}{16}, \\\\ \\pm \\frac{4}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{4}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{4}{4}, & \\pm \\frac{4}{8}, & \\pm \\frac{4}{16}, \\\\ \\pm \\frac{6}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{6}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{6}{4}, & \\pm \\frac{6}{8}, & \\pm \\frac{6}{16}, \\\\ \\pm \\frac{12}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{12}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{12}{4}, & \\pm \\frac{12}{8}, & \\pm \\frac{12}{16}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we reduce the fractions to their lowest terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\pm 1, & \\pm \\frac{1}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{4}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{8}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{16}, \\\\ \\pm 2, & \\pm 1, & \\pm \\frac{1}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{4}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{8}, \\\\ \\pm 3, & \\pm \\frac{3}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{3}{4}, & \\pm \\frac{3}{8}, & \\pm \\frac{3}{16}, \\\\ \\pm 4, & \\pm 2, & \\pm 1, & \\pm \\frac{1}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{4}, \\\\ \\pm 6, & \\pm 3, & \\pm \\frac{3}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{3}{4}, & \\pm \\frac{3}{8}, \\\\ \\pm 12, & \\pm 6, & \\pm 3, & \\pm \\frac{3}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{3}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that some potential roots are duplicates (shown above in red). Removing these duplicates, and listing those remaining in order from least to greatest in absolute value, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\pm \\frac{1}{16},\\text{ }\\pm \\frac{1}{8},\\text{ }\\pm \\frac{3}{16},\\text{ }\\pm \\frac{1}{4},\\text{ }\\pm \\frac{3}{8},\\text{ }\\pm \\frac{1}{2},\\text{ }\\pm \\frac{3}{4}, \\\\  & \\pm 1,\\text{ }\\pm \\frac{3}{2},\\text{ }\\pm 2,\\text{ }\\pm 3,\\text{ }\\pm 4,\\text{ }\\pm 6,\\text{ }\\pm 12.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis makes a total of $28$ potential roots. (Remember that each $\\pm $ sign accounts for $2$ roots, and $2\\cdot 14=28.$)"} :step/id #uuid "1c78b6e5-0b3d-542f-981c-72db0b806463" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492943}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492943 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492877 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "502dce7e-39fd-5d3e-90fe-883c9d4faed3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492370}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492370} :answer-field/id #uuid "0f5435d7-226a-5cc7-911d-fda5e505d743" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492710}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "7649552c-e2dd-537f-80f0-06e90c2af71c" :question/math-academy-id "q-323223" :question/problem "According to the rational root theorem, the polynomial $f(x)=4{x}^{4}-9{x}^{2}+3$ has{{field-1}} *potential* rational roots." :question/worked-solution "Given a polynomial with integer coefficients, the rational roots theorem states that any rational roots of the polynomial must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of the constant term}}{\\text{factor of the leading coefficient}}.\n$$\n\nFor the given polynomial:\n\n- The constant term is $3,$ and its factors are $\\pm 1$,  $\\pm 3.$\n\n- The leading coefficient is $4,$ and its factors are $\\pm 1$, $\\pm 2,$ and $\\pm 4.$\n\nFirst, we list out all the possibilities for the numerator and denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\pm \\frac{1}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{4}, & \\pm \\frac{3}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{3}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{3}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we reduce the fractions to lowest terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\pm 1, & \\pm \\frac{1}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{4}, & \\pm 3, & \\pm \\frac{3}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{3}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that none of these potential roots are duplicates. Listing those potential roots in order from least to greatest in absolute value, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\pm \\frac{1}{4}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{3}{4}, & \\pm 1, & \\pm \\frac{3}{2}, & \\pm 3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis makes a total of $12$ potential roots. (Remember that each $\\pm $ sign accounts for $2$ roots, and $2\\cdot 6=12.$)"} :step/id #uuid "3d4f4a1a-0d09-50c6-9d90-b5ac235d5b30" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492962}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492949 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186433674 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "14dbd20f-dae6-536f-87a7-89593f714dee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433636} {:answer/id #uuid "7c44e16a-680d-5060-8d29-321f46d27bfa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433647} {:answer/id #uuid "8b19fd5b-b890-57f9-8edb-92c8075ccdef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433649} {:answer/id #uuid "d26b2ae3-d848-5cfd-a798-719a96527d83" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433659} {:answer/id #uuid "e42f90f8-5654-57c3-8684-e319bb32b08d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433662}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186433649} :answer-field/id #uuid "82b73eeb-28f6-544a-b4a5-e943633f0b85" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433669}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "b75db326-1476-547f-a77b-b21ea5825d85" :question/math-academy-id "q-83824" :question/problem "According to the rational roots theorem, which of the following **cannot** be a root of the polynomial $f(x)=5{x}^{4}+{x}^{2}-7x+8?$" :question/worked-solution "Given a polynomial with integer coefficients, the rational roots theorem states that any rational roots of the polynomial must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of the constant term}}{\\text{factor of the leading coefficient}}.\n$$\n\nFor the given polynomial $f(x),$ the constant term is $8$ and the leading coefficient is $5.$ So, any rational roots of $f(x)$ must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of }8}{\\text{factor of }5}.\n$$\n\nOf the given answer choices, only $-5$ does not take the above form. The value $-5$ can be interpreted as a fraction $-\\frac{5}{1},$ and the numerator $(5)$ is not a factor of $8.$"} :step/id #uuid "49d109c1-966d-5d06-a1b4-37f9036c2497" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492935}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492961 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335887 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3916b332-b1bf-56dd-ba24-217edc46155a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333522} {:answer/id #uuid "69ad4502-e492-59c7-a566-d06801cfd838" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333955} {:answer/id #uuid "75a12632-6342-5324-a3b6-e62706c87939" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334051} {:answer/id #uuid "786075ed-ee83-5fb6-a116-f1eab63b1476" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334069} {:answer/id #uuid "fcb95b3c-af6d-5fd3-b35b-7b81469d3cd8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335209}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186333955} :answer-field/id #uuid "6502c1b8-0bfa-5633-9f9c-b5117737a077" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335435}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "6d02cc2f-0a59-5f12-841d-8b0228cd0395" :question/math-academy-id "q-6657" :question/problem "According to the rational roots theorem, which of the following **cannot** be a root of the polynomial $f(x)=2{x}^{3}+{x}^{2}-2x-1?$" :question/worked-solution "Given a polynomial with integer coefficients, the rational roots theorem states that any rational roots of the polynomial must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of the constant term}}{\\text{factor of the leading coefficient}}.\n$$\n\nFor the given polynomial $f(x),$ the constant term is $-1$ and the leading coefficient is $2.$ So, any rational roots of $f(x)$ must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of }-1}{\\text{factor of }2}.\n$$\n\nOf the given answer choices, only $2$ does not take the above form. The value $2$ can be interpreted as a fraction $\\frac{2}{1},$ and the numerator $(2)$ is not a factor of $-1.$"} :step/id #uuid "7bbb91cb-0115-59be-b84d-383083ba59ce" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492949}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492962 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492830 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d17d593-2ae4-53d7-a4b5-82e61d1bfa57" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492236}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492236} :answer-field/id #uuid "d1ef24a4-cbc2-5e7b-841f-6bdc135e149a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492791}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "12d33f18-863a-5b25-ace7-f97482164c70" :question/math-academy-id "q-323572" :question/problem "Given that the polynomial $f(x)={x}^{3}-{x}^{2}-x-15$ has a root in the interval $(1,4),$ the sum of the **distinct** real roots of $f(x)$ equals{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "Given a polynomial with integer coefficients, the rational roots theorem states that any rational roots of the polynomial must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of the constant term}}{\\text{factor of the leading coefficient}}.\n$$\n\nFor the given polynomial $f(x),$ the constant term is $-15$ and the leading coefficient is $1.$ So, any rational roots of $f(x)$ must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of }15}{\\text{factor of }1}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the possible rational roots are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\pm 1,\\,\\pm 3,\\,\\pm 5,\\,\\pm 15.\n$$\n\nOf these roots, the only root in the interval $(1,4)$ is $3.$\n\nNow, let's use synthetic division to test whether $(x-3)$ is a factor of $f(x).$ We get the following:\n\n|  | ${x}^{3}$ | ${x}^{2}$ | ${x}^{1}$ | ${x}^{0}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $3$ | $1$ | $-1$ | $-1$ | $-15$ |\n|  | $↓$ | $3$ | $6$ | $15$ |\n|  | $1$ | $2$ | $5$ | $0$ |\n\nSince the remainder is $0,$ we conclude that $(x-3)$ is indeed a factor of $f(x).$ Moreover, we have\n\n$$\nf(x)=(x-3)({x}^{2}+2x+5).\n$$\n\nNow we must see if the polynomial $g(x)={x}^{2}+2x+5$ has real roots. For this, we must find the value of its discriminant $D.$ Note that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & ={b}^{2}-4ac \\\\  & ={2}^{2}-4(1)(5) \\\\  & =4-20 \\\\  & =-16.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $D<0,$ the polynomial $g(x)$ doesn't have real roots. \n\nTherefore, the only root of $f(x)$ is $x=3.$"} :step/id #uuid "82d25e83-31f3-50b5-8226-00ac5920f5f0"}] :activity/title "The Rational Roots Theorem" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492205}]}
